  "worked_solution": "Note that the factor $2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ is always positive for all real numbers $x.$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9})(-7x-5)}{\\,2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\,} & <\\frac{0}{\\,2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\,} \\\\ -7x-5 & <0 \\\\ -7x & <5 \\\\ x & >\\,-\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
